<!--
  BlockML Core — bootstrap documentation

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.documentation.CapabilitySemantics
-->
<block
  xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
  xsi:schemaLocation="http://blockml.org/bml https://blockml.org/schemas/bml.xsd
                      "
  xmlns:cap="org.blockml.bml.capability"
  xmlns:core="org.blockml.bml.core"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:lang="org.blockml.bml.language">

  <name>CapabilitySemantics</name>
  <type>doc:CapabilitySemantics</type>
  <baseType>core:Documentation</baseType>
  <is>A guide to Capability meaning</is>

  <documentation>
    BlockML describes semantics only. There are no special language concepts for constructor,
    getter, setter, or render — all behaviour is expressed as ordinary Capabilities: init(),
    getValue(), setValue(), render(), and domain-specific operations. Package:
    org.blockml.bml.capability.* (base org.blockml.bml.core.Capability).

    Capability kinds describe intent — they are not compiler rules or runtime objects:
    • Inbound — receives input from outside (setValue, configure); requires inputType; no outputType
    • Outbound — produces output to outside (getValue, emit); requires outputType; no inputType
    • Transformation — maps input to output (compute, format, render); requires both
    Async reply via replyTo is a runtime/message pattern — not modeled on the Capability.

    Unqualified Capability type resolves to org.blockml.bml.capability.*; unqualified inputType /
    outputType resolve to org.blockml.bml.type.* (doc:ImplicitNamespaceResolution). General block
    references (aggregation type, association type, baseType) have no implicit namespace.

    Authoring: declare under &lt;capabilities&gt; with a camelCase name (visibility prefixes apply —
    doc:MemberVisibility). In a Composition the Capability value is the address or binding —
    attribute on the child Block instance, like a Property value. Private Capabilities (__name)
    cannot be bound from outside the declaring Block; protected (_name) follow the formal access
    rule including derived Blocks and the direct composition owner.

    Each Capability may carry an implementation whose language attribute references a Language FQN
    (org.blockml.bml.language.*). utility Sub-Blocks (baseType Entity) commonly host Transformation
    implementations — doc:SubBlockModel. Preferred semantic dialect: PseudoCode
    (doc:PseudoCode). The renderer decides how to realise each Capability in the target system:

    init()     → Java constructor · UI5 onInit() · React useEffect()
    getValue() → direct field access · generated getter · reflection
    setValue() → direct field assignment · generated setter
    render()   → SCAD module · JSX component · may be ignored

    Interpretation happens only in the renderer — by reading Capability definitions and member
    values from the immutable BOM. The BOM never contains implementation concepts such as
    constructors, getters, setters, runtime values, or executable behaviour.

    A Block's public surface is Capabilities — internal structure (Properties, Aggregations)
    is not the outward API; the renderer decides concrete manifestation.
  </documentation>

  <references>
    <see type="doc:ImplicitNamespaceResolution" relation="SeeAlso">
      Unqualified Capability type vs inputType / outputType sites.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:SubBlockModel" relation="SeeAlso">
      utility Sub-Blocks hosting Transformation implementations.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:PseudoCode" relation="SeeAlso">
      Preferred Capability implementation dialect for semantic prose.
    </see>
    <see type="core:Capability" relation="Extends">
      Capability branch — implementation member and input/output type rules.
    </see>
    <see type="cap:Inbound" relation="Defines">
      Inbound capability kind — receives external input.
    </see>
    <see type="cap:Outbound" relation="Defines">
      Outbound capability kind — produces external output.
    </see>
    <see type="cap:Transformation" relation="Defines">
      Transformation capability kind — maps input to output.
    </see>
    <see type="lang:PseudoCode" relation="SeeAlso">
      Free-form pseudocode — preferred for semantic descriptions readable by LLMs.
    </see>
    <see type="doc:Expressions" relation="ReadNext">
      Next reading step — dynamic values and bean calls in expressions.
    </see>
  </references>

</block>
